  [1mApproach A — one file, inline script, no modules, no build.[0m
  Everything in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. Zero tooling, zero install, zero concepts. Wins when the app stays truly tiny and you want
  to read the whole thing at once. Costs: no real unit testing, and one file holding storage, rendering, and scheduling
  gets tangled fast — which is exactly the file that grows. [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mApproach B — vanilla ES modules, served locally, no framework, no bundler.[0m
  Three focused modules — [38;5;153mstore.js[39m (load/save/CRUD), [38;5;153mreminders.js[39m (deciding what's due and firing), [38;5;153mui.js[39m (render +
  events) — plus [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. Each is independently testable with Vitest running in Node; no build step, no framework, no
  bundler. Wins when you want the pieces separable and the reminder logic tested without a browser, which matters here
  because "did it fire at the right time" is the part most likely to be subtly wrong. Costs: a dev server command, and
  three files instead of one. [3mComplexity: trivial-to-moderate.[0m

  [1mApproach C — Vite plus a framework (React or Svelte).[0m
  Component model, HMR, testing and linting configured by convention. Wins if this grows into something substantial or
  you already think in components. Costs: a build step, a dependency tree, and more framework concepts than the app has
  actual logic — for four fields and a timer, the scaffolding would outweigh the program. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mI recommend B.[0m The interesting, bug-prone part of this app is the reminder logic — clock handling, what happens across
  sleep, not firing the same reminder twice. B is the cheapest approach that lets me test that logic directly, in
  isolation, without a browser or a build. A can't test it; C tests it fine but makes you pay for a toolchain to get
  there. B is also trivially upgradeable to C later — the modules become the logic layer and only [38;5;153mui.js[39m gets replaced.
